WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.116
This is Read Tabs, Play Guitar, by Fretbox. Guitar tab and notation, one step at a time.

00:00:07.916 --> 00:00:10.423
Today: the fretboard and open chords.

00:00:12.223 --> 00:00:14.559
The fretboard. Finding the notes.

00:00:15.359 --> 00:00:26.861
Tab tells you where to put your fingers, but knowing the notes on the fretboard turns a list of numbers into music you understand. You do not need to memorise every note at once. Start with a few landmarks.

00:00:28.361 --> 00:00:29.629
The musical alphabet.

00:00:30.429 --> 00:01:00.530
Western music uses seven letter names: A, B, C, D, E, F, G. Between most of them there is a note in between, a sharp (#) or flat (b): A#/Bb, C#/Db, D#/Eb, F#/Gb, G#/Ab. There are two exceptions: there is no note between B and C, and none between E and F. Those pairs are one semitone apart.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.235
So the twelve notes, in semitones, are:

00:01:05.035 --> 00:01:15.918
A, A#, B, C, C#, D, D#, E, F, F#, G, G#, and then A again.

00:01:17.418 --> 00:01:18.895
Notes on the low E string.

00:01:19.695 --> 00:01:27.218
Because each fret is a semitone, you can work out every note on a string by counting from the open note. On string 6 (E):

00:01:28.018 --> 00:01:29.420
Fret 0: E.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:31.646
Fret 1: F.

00:01:32.446 --> 00:01:33.841
Fret 3: G.

00:01:34.641 --> 00:01:36.167
Fret 5: A.

00:01:36.967 --> 00:01:38.397
Fret 7: B.

00:01:39.197 --> 00:01:40.506
Fret 8: C.

00:01:41.306 --> 00:01:42.615
Fret 10: D.

00:01:43.415 --> 00:01:45.076
Fret 12: E again.

00:01:45.876 --> 00:01:49.857
String 1 has the same notes at the same frets, two octaves higher.

00:01:51.357 --> 00:01:52.611
Notes on the A string.

00:01:53.411 --> 00:01:55.016
On string 5 (A):

00:01:55.816 --> 00:01:57.284
Fret 0: A.

00:01:58.084 --> 00:01:59.411
Fret 2: B.

00:02:00.211 --> 00:02:01.512
Fret 3: C.

00:02:02.312 --> 00:02:03.743
Fret 5: D.

00:02:04.543 --> 00:02:05.913
Fret 7: E.

00:02:06.713 --> 00:02:08.056
Fret 8: F.

00:02:08.856 --> 00:02:10.244
Fret 10: G.

00:02:11.044 --> 00:02:12.745
Fret 12: A again.

00:02:13.545 --> 00:02:19.184
These two strings are the most useful to learn first, because many chords and scale shapes start on them.

00:02:20.684 --> 00:02:21.775
Octave shapes.

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:37.105
A quick way to find the same note on another string: from any note on string 6, move two strings across (to string 4) and two frets up. That is the same note, one octave higher. The same works from string 5 to string 3.

00:02:37.905 --> 00:02:45.939
Tip: Say the note names out loud as you play. It feels slow at first, but within a few weeks you will know the low strings without thinking.

00:02:47.439 --> 00:02:48.160
Practice.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:54.127
First: play the notes of string 6 from fret 0 to 12, saying each name.

00:02:54.927 --> 00:02:57.453
Second: do the same on string 5.

00:02:58.253 --> 00:03:06.438
Third: pick a note, for example G, and find it on strings 6 and 5. Then find its octave on strings 4 and 3.

00:03:08.238 --> 00:03:11.807
Open chords. The shapes behind thousands of songs.

00:03:12.607 --> 00:03:24.420
A chord is three or more notes played together. Open chords mix fretted notes with open strings, and they are the first chords most guitarists learn. With a handful of them you can play a huge number of songs.

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:27.224
Reading a chord in tab.

00:03:28.024 --> 00:03:39.875
In tab, a chord is a vertical stack of numbers. An x means do not play that string. Here are seven essential open chords, written from string 1 (top) to string 6 (bottom):

00:03:40.675 --> 00:03:42.666
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:03:44.166 --> 00:03:45.191
Major and minor.

00:03:45.991 --> 00:04:03.382
Compare E and Em, or A and Am: only one note changes. That note is the third of the chord. In a major chord it is four semitones above the root; in a minor chord it is three. Major chords tend to sound bright, and minor chords darker, but the context of the song matters too.

00:04:04.882 --> 00:04:05.990
Changing chords.

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:12.723
The hard part is not holding a chord; it is changing from one to the next in time. Some habits that help:

00:04:13.523 --> 00:04:20.211
Look for anchor fingers: a finger that stays on the same string and fret in both chords, or slides along one string.

00:04:21.011 --> 00:04:23.901
Move all your fingers as a group, not one at a time.

00:04:24.701 --> 00:04:31.800
Practise the change, not the chord. Go back and forth between two chords, slowly, without strumming in between.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:43.899
Tip: If a string buzzes or is muted, check three things in order: is the fingertip close to the fret, is it pressing on its tip so it does not touch the next string, and is your thumb low enough behind the neck?

00:04:45.399 --> 00:04:46.120
Practice.

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:53.821
First: play each chord one string at a time, from the lowest string you should play to string 1. Every note should ring.

00:04:54.621 --> 00:05:00.608
Second: change between Em and G for one minute, then C and G, then D and A.

00:05:01.408 --> 00:05:07.890
Third: strum each chord four times, then change, keeping a steady beat. Start very slowly.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:14.906
Fourth: play G, C, D, Em in a loop. Many songs use these four chords.

00:05:16.706 --> 00:05:17.767
That's all for today.

00:05:18.567 --> 00:05:19.769
If you want to keep going:

00:05:20.569 --> 00:05:31.981
Fretbox is the app behind the ebook. It is a tab player for guitarists: it opens tablature files in the Guitar Pro and MusicXML formats, so you can read a song and hear it at the same time.

00:05:32.781 --> 00:05:35.418
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to be used with it:

00:05:36.218 --> 00:05:38.978
reading tablature and the symbols for each technique.

00:05:39.778 --> 00:05:42.377
following the rhythm in the notation that comes with the tab.

00:05:43.177 --> 00:05:45.908
learning the fretboard, chords and scales.

00:05:46.708 --> 00:05:51.022
practising song by song, section by section, at your own pace.

00:05:51.822 --> 00:05:55.967
This ebook is free. Share it with anyone who is starting out on the guitar.

00:05:56.767 --> 00:06:01.328
